\begin{blockquote}
Does your data contain any duplicate test IDs and are you interested only in the unique tests? (ie. does each test get its own label?) You might get better performance by doing the filtering before splitting the corpus into labels and sentences instead of doing the splitting first and only filtering after. I used the default split with default vocabulary (word count:496k). You might consider the tokenizer from nltk instead of word
-- tokenizer_word is a more verbose tokenizer based on the 30k common words dictionary which would provide you more meaningful tokens.  If all you need is the words the 496k corpus is fine already.
I also used a stemmer just for convenience
- The sentence_splitter tokenizer. The same stemming filter would be better before the splitting, but that might be too computationally expensive. If all you need is the words the tokenizer alone is sufficient.
The splitter needs some more tuning if you want to optimize precision as you have to specify the maximum length and an
-- id to use in the tokenizer for duplicate test IDs. You can use 8 for maximum length and a dummy string for the id. Another possible option would be to increase your maximum sentence length, but then you'll need to do some experiments to tune the results to your satisfaction.
One possibility might be also to skip the splitter altogether and write a regular expression to remove duplicate strings.
-- if there are only a couple of thousands strings you should have enough power to filter them all even by hand. For a few millions duplicates this should not even be attempted.
-- I took the filter out since it makes my implementation too clumsy for large texts. You can also use the following regular expression:
`(?=\\P{L}).+(?=\\P{L})`
but there will definitely be some problems you can't predict and need to look at the sample code.
\end{blockquote}

